//? Title: Variational Autoencoder: Explicit Gaussian Codes and a Bernoulli Decoder
//? Subtitle: A two-dimensional posterior, pathwise derivatives and 96 visible Adam steps
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Generating an image from a code requires a distribution over codes and a decoder that works
without its target. This report fits a two-coordinate Gaussian encoder and a Bernoulli decoder
on 1,000 statically thresholded MNIST images, using 200 validation and 200 test records.
All 96 Adam updates remain editable C++. Four fixed posterior draws select epoch 3;
test reconstruction NLL plus analytic KL is 205.507103 nats per image. Decode(mu) pixel MSE
is 0.080653, compared with 0.086690 for the fitting-only binary mean. Pathwise checks,
unfiltered images and a code-only decoder explain what this short teaching run establishes.
)
\toc

> A distribution over codes, then an image distribution given a code

[Kingma and Welling's Auto-Encoding Variational Bayes](https://arxiv.org/abs/1312.6114)
uses a tractable approximate posterior and reparameterized samples to optimize a variational
objective. The encoder here maps 784 binary pixels through 64 ReLU units to two means and
two log variances. Explicit standard-normal epsilon gives z=mu+exp(log_variance/2)*epsilon.
The decoder maps z through 64 ReLU units to 784 Bernoulli logits. No pixels bypass the code.
Independent standard-normal prior codes use exactly the same decoder.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/vanilla_vae_method.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::graphics::Diagram          d(47, 1100);
  dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle st;
  st.width     = 5.2;
  st.height    = 2.7;
  st.font_size = 20;
  d.node("x", {0, 0}, "Binary image x\n784 pixels", st)
      .node("e", {7, 0}, "Encoder\nReLU64 / mu2, logv2", st)
      .node("z", {14, 0}, "Posterior code z\nExplicit epsilon2", st)
      .node("g", {21, 0}, "Shared decoder\nReLU64 / logits784", st)
      .node("o", {28, 0}, "Bernoulli probabilities\nsigmoid(logits)", st)
      .node("p", {14, 6}, "Independent prior code\nN(0,I), width2", st)
      .node("gp", {21, 6}, "SAME decoder\nCode-only generation", st)
      .node("k", {7, -6}, "Analytic KL\nq(z|x) to N(0,I)", st)
      .edge("x", "e")
      .edge("e", "z")
      .edge("z", "g")
      .edge("g", "o")
      .edge("p", "gp")
      .edge("e", "k")
      .text(
          {14, -10},
          "One explicit loss: batch mean of pixel NLL plus Gaussian KL.\nDecoder weights are "
          "shared; no image or label enters prior decoding.",
          20)
      .save_svg("build/vanilla_vae_method.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
\image(figures/vanilla_vae_method.svg)
// result_end

\caption(The sampled two-coordinate code is the only connection from encoder to decoder. Prior generation needs neither encoder nor target image.)
\alttext(Binary pixels enter a Gaussian encoder, explicit noise samples its code, and the shared decoder supplies Bernoulli probabilities. A separate prior code uses the same decoder. Analytic KL regularizes the posterior.)

For p(z)=N(0,I) and q(z|x)=N(mu,diag(exp(l))), Jensen's inequality gives
log p(x) >= E_q[log p(x|z)] - KL(q||p). The Bernoulli decoder and diagonal Gaussian make the
per-image terms explicit:

$$
z_{bd}=\mu_{bd}+e^{l_{bd}/2}\epsilon_{bd},\quad \epsilon_{bd}\sim N(0,1),\\
R_b=\sum_{j=1}^{784}[\operatorname{softplus}(a_{bj})-x_{bj}a_{bj}],\qquad
K_b=\frac12\sum_{d=1}^{2}[\mu_{bd}^2+e^{l_{bd}}-1-l_{bd}],\\
L=\frac1B\sum_{b=1}^{B}(R_b+K_b).
$$
\alttext(A Gaussian sample is a differentiable function of mean, log variance and explicit noise. Stable Bernoulli pixel NLL sums 784 terms; analytic Gaussian KL sums two. Only the image batch is averaged.)

This is the native BetaVariationalAutoencoder with beta=1, feature width 784, latent width 2
and hidden width 64. There is no KL annealing. The finite Monte Carlo negative ELBO estimate
is not an exact image log likelihood or an importance-weighted objective, and each realized
sample need not bound the likelihood. Dense forward work is O(BH(F+L)); weights are point
estimates and the posterior is diagonal. The core owns the math, while the caller owns
noise, batches, checkpoint selection and optimizer steps.

> Verify the pathwise derivatives before fitting

A controlled two-row example checks every mean, log-variance and epsilon derivative of
mean_b[0.5 sum_d z_bd^2+K_b]. With B=2, these derivatives are (z+mu)/2,
[0.5 z sigma epsilon+0.5(exp(l)-1)]/2 and z sigma/2, respectively.
The two lines below vary epsilon at fixed mu=0.2 and sigma=1 or 2; they are a controlled
sampling illustration, rather than fitted decoder predictions.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/vanilla_vae_pathwise.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace n = dm::nn;
  const std::vector<double> m{.2, -.4, .1, .3}, l{0, std::log(4.), std::log(.25), .2},
      e{1, -.5, 2, -1};
  auto mu         = n::from_vector(m, {2, 2}).requires_grad_(),
       lv         = n::from_vector(l, {2, 2}).requires_grad_(),
       eps        = n::from_vector(e, {2, 2}).requires_grad_();
  const auto z    = n::models::GaussianReparameterize(mu, lv, eps);
  const auto loss = n::mean(.5 * n::sum(n::square(z), {1}) + n::models::DiagonalGaussianKl(mu, lv));
  const auto g    = n::grad(loss, {mu, lv, eps});
  const auto values = z.to_vector<double>();
  std::print(
      "| coordinate | sampled z | d mean | d log variance | d epsilon "
      "|\n|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n");
  for (int i = 0; i < 4; ++i) {
    const double              sigma = std::exp(l[i] / 2), v = m[i] + sigma * e[i];
    const std::vector<double> expected{
        (v + m[i]) / 2, (.5 * v * sigma * e[i] + .5 * (std::exp(l[i]) - 1)) / 2, v * sigma / 2};
    if (std::abs(values[i] - v) > 1e-14)
      return 1;
    for (int k = 0; k < 3; ++k)
      if (std::abs(g[k].to_vector<double>()[i] - expected[k]) > 1e-14)
        return 1;
    std::print(
        "| {} | {:.6f} | {:.6f} | {:.6f} | {:.6f} |\n",
        i,
        values[i],
        g[0].to_vector<double>()[i],
        g[1].to_vector<double>()[i],
        g[2].to_vector<double>()[i]);
  }
  const auto extreme = n::models::BernoulliPixelNll(
      n::tensor({{100., -100., 100., -100.}}), n::tensor({{1., 0., 0., 1.}}));
  if (std::abs(extreme.item() - 200) > 1e-12 ||
      n::models::DiagonalGaussianKl(n::zeros({1, 2}), n::zeros({1, 2})).item() != 0)
    return 1;
  auto                chart = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  std::vector<double> noise;
  for (int i = 0; i <= 40; ++i)
    noise.push_back(-2. + i * .1);
  for (int k = 0; k < 2; ++k) {
    const double sigma  = k == 0 ? 1 : 2;
    const auto   output = n::models::GaussianReparameterize(
        n::full({41, 1}, .2, n::DType::Float64),
        n::full({41, 1}, 2 * std::log(sigma), n::DType::Float64),
        n::from_vector(noise, {41, 1}));
    chart.lines(
        noise,
        output.to_vector<double>(),
        k == 0 ? "sigma 1" : "sigma 2",
        k == 0 ? dm::RGBA{37, 99, 235} : dm::RGBA{220, 38, 38},
        2);
  }
  chart.title("Controlled reparameterization / mu = 0.2")
      .x_label("explicit epsilon")
      .y_label("sampled latent coordinate")
      .save_svg("build/vanilla_vae_pathwise.svg");
}
```
